<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etelköz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Atelkouzou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Etelköz was the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, where the Hungarians lived in the 9th century before their conquest of the Carpathian Basin. It is named in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>. Around 895 attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> drove the Hungarians west across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etelkoz.jpg" alt="Etelköz" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lázár deák (Lazarus secretarius) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tabula_hungariae.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
